This week I learned a lot about web security and why it is something developers need to think about from the beginning of a project. 
The topic that stood out to me the most was the OWASP Top 10, especially A01: Broken Access Control. 
I learned that this happens when users can access information or features they are not supposed to. 
It made me realize that even if a website works correctly, it can still have security problems if access is not managed properly.

I also learned that using HTTPS is not enough to fully protect a website. 
There are other security features like Content Security Policy (CSP), SameSite cookies, and CSRF tokens that help prevent different types of attacks. 
Before this week, I had heard some of these terms, but I did not really understand what they were used for. 
Now I have a better idea of how they help make websites more secure.

Overall, this week's lesson showed me that security is not just one feature that you add at the end. 
It needs to be part of the entire development process. 
As I continue learning web development, I want to keep these security practices in mind so I can build applications that are not only functional but also safe for users.
